    <div style="background:#10233d;padding:20px;border-radius:14px;margin-bottom:20px;border:1px solid #33415c;border-left:4px solid #22d3ee;">
    <h3 style="color:#f8bbd0;margin-top:0;">🔭 Biblical and Historical Background</h3>
    <p><strong>Faith in God:</strong> David faced Goliath trusting in “the name of the Lord Almighty” (1 Samuel 17:45). <strong>Courage:</strong> as a young shepherd he volunteered to fight Goliath (1 Samuel 17:32). <strong>Humility:</strong> he was a shepherd boy who did not seek the throne (1 Samuel 16:11). <strong>Respect for authority:</strong> he spared King Saul’s life twice, refusing to harm “the Lord’s anointed” (1 Samuel 24:6; 26). <strong>Worship:</strong> he danced before the Lord with all his might (2 Samuel 6:14) and wrote psalms. <strong>Justice:</strong> he did what was just and right for all his people (2 Samuel 8:15). <strong>Repentance and accepting correction:</strong> David sinned by taking Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah, and arranging Uriah’s death. When the prophet Nathan confronted him, David admitted, “I have sinned against the Lord” (2 Samuel 12:13) and prayed for a clean heart (Psalm 51). <strong>Why “a man after God’s own heart”?</strong> He loved and trusted God, sought God’s guidance, worshipped sincerely, obeyed God in many things and, when he sinned, repented fully. <strong>General qualities of good leaders:</strong> integrity, humility, courage, fairness, accountability, good communication, vision, teamwork and service.</p>
    </div>
